  "interpretation": "Manipulation check (mean_dose_delivered, default - no_memory): point=0.00315, CI=[0.00276, 0.00358] over n_seeds=15 -- excludes zero, confirming the mechanism actually delivers a nonzero starting record_quality to successor delegates at default parameters, as designed. REFINED (not simply confirmed/refuted) finding on Level 1.1's 'negligible effect at default parameters' claim, mean_end_capacity (default - no_memory): point=0.00038, CI=[0.00030, 0.00047] over n_seeds=15 -- this seed-clustered paired design is powerful enough that even this tiny effect is STATISTICALLY DETECTABLE (the CI excludes zero, entirely on the positive side) -- so 'negligible' is not literally 'no effect'. But its PRACTICAL magnitude relative to the no_memory baseline capacity (0.1726) is 0.22% -- a real, monotonic, dose-scaling effect (moderate: point=0.00097; high_retention: point=0.00270; both also exclude zero) that is nonetheless practically trivial at the parameter values Level 1.1 actually used. The correct, precise restatement of Level 1.1's finding is therefore: institutional memory is NOT a broken/inert mechanism -- it is a real, working, monotonic dose-response -- but its default calibration places it at a practically negligible (not statistically zero) operating point. Ceiling check (mean_end_capacity, max_dose - no_memory, retention=1.0/transfer=1.0): point=0.04318, CI=[0.03622, 0.04985] over n_seeds=15 (25.0% relative to baseline) -- confirms the mechanism CAN produce a practically non-trivial effect at a large enough dose; the default's smallness is a calibration choice, not a structural ceiling the mechanism cannot exceed. A plausible (not confirmed by this script) contributing factor for why even max_dose's relative effect stays under 25% rather than larger: recall-triggered turnover disproportionately removes delegates who committed misconduct, whose own record_quality was often already low or decayed at removal -- so the 'memory' being passed down skews toward low-quality predecessors regardless of retention/transfer, independent of the multiplicative retention*transfer attenuation. See docs/level1.1b_validation_note.md for the confirmatory read and every intermediate condition's own numbers."
